1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:07,862
Here is an adder.

2
00:00:07,862 --> 00:00:13,444
Put two numbers on its inputs, and their sum comes out on the other side.

3
00:00:13,544 --> 00:00:19,906
It has no memory at all, so its output depends only on what the inputs are right now.

4
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,962
Let's give it a job that sounds easy.

5
00:00:22,962 --> 00:00:32,156
A list of numbers arrives one at a time, three, then one, then four, then two, and we want their total.

6
00:00:32,256 --> 00:00:38,376
The total is ten, but the adder only ever sees two numbers at once.

7
00:00:38,816 --> 00:00:45,724
So one input has to be the next number from the list, and the other has to be the total so far.

8
00:00:45,824 --> 00:00:49,764
And the total so far is exactly what the adder produces.

9
00:00:49,764 --> 00:00:56,446
The natural thing to do is to run a wire from the output straight back to the second input.

10
00:00:56,883 --> 00:00:59,015
On paper this looks finished.

11
00:00:59,015 --> 00:01:04,033
To find out whether it works, we have to watch what the wires do over time,

12
00:01:04,033 --> 00:01:06,709
and for that we need a way to draw time.

13
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:11,064
Take a single wire and touch a probe to it.

14
00:01:11,064 --> 00:01:16,916
All the probe can measure is a voltage, so we plot that voltage against time.

15
00:01:17,016 --> 00:01:20,598
A picture like this is called a waveform.

16
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:24,644
Most of the time the voltage sits at one of two levels.

17
00:01:24,644 --> 00:01:30,536
By convention the low level means a zero and the high level means a one.

18
00:01:30,636 --> 00:01:35,892
So this wire said zero, then one, then zero again.

19
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:39,508
Look closely and the levels are not perfect.

20
00:01:39,508 --> 00:01:45,624
A high is sometimes a little short of the top, and a low floats a little above the bottom.

21
00:01:45,724 --> 00:01:46,583
That is fine,

22
00:01:46,583 --> 00:01:50,486
because every gate reads anything near the top as a one and

23
00:01:50,486 --> 00:01:53,794
then drives its own output all the way to the top.

24
00:01:53,794 --> 00:01:58,166
The small errors are wiped out at each gate instead of piling up,

25
00:01:58,166 --> 00:02:00,924
and that is what makes a circuit digital.

26
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:05,752
A number needs several wires, one for each bit.

27
00:02:05,752 --> 00:02:11,118
Here are four of them carrying the number three, and then the number six.

28
00:02:11,218 --> 00:02:15,632
Drawing every wire gets crowded, so we draw the whole group,

29
00:02:15,632 --> 00:02:19,752
called a bus, as one band with its value written inside.

30
00:02:19,752 --> 00:02:23,960
Where the band pinches, the value is changing.

31
00:02:25,183 --> 00:02:27,707
Now watch the adder in this picture.

32
00:02:27,707 --> 00:02:32,413
Both inputs are zero, so the output is zero.

33
00:02:32,513 --> 00:02:35,091
Change the top input to three.

34
00:02:35,091 --> 00:02:40,013
The output does become three, but not at the same moment.

35
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:44,352
The change has to work its way through the transistors inside.

36
00:02:44,352 --> 00:02:50,542
The time from an input changing to the output settling is called the propagation delay.

37
00:02:50,642 --> 00:02:54,842
Let's say that for this adder it is two nanoseconds.

38
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:00,469
With that we can test our circuit.

39
00:03:00,469 --> 00:03:07,179
The total starts at zero, and at time zero the first number, three, arrives.

40
00:03:07,279 --> 00:03:13,951
The numbers come one every eight nanoseconds, so the three stays on the input until then.

41
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:20,571
Two nanoseconds later the adder has worked out zero plus three, and the output says three.

42
00:03:20,571 --> 00:03:23,975
That is the correct total so far.

43
00:03:24,416 --> 00:03:30,779
But the output is wired back to the input, so now the adder sees three plus three,

44
00:03:30,779 --> 00:03:33,728
and two nanoseconds later it says six.

45
00:03:33,828 --> 00:03:39,604
Then six plus three makes nine, and nine plus three makes twelve.

46
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:44,540
Only now, at eight nanoseconds, does the second number show up.

47
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:49,034
The total should still be three, ready to become four.

48
00:03:49,134 --> 00:03:56,038
Instead the wire says twelve, and it carries on to thirteen, fourteen, fifteen.

49
00:03:56,483 --> 00:04:02,157
Nothing is broken here, because the adder did exactly what an adder does.

50
00:04:02,257 --> 00:04:07,578
The trouble is that each new sum went back into the input the moment it was ready,

51
00:04:07,578 --> 00:04:10,303
and nothing in the circuit ever said wait.

52
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:13,502
So let's say what we need.

53
00:04:13,502 --> 00:04:17,854
Somewhere on that feedback wire there should be a box that holds on

54
00:04:17,854 --> 00:04:21,168
to the old total and keeps showing it to the adder.

55
00:04:21,268 --> 00:04:26,770
The new sum can wait at the door, but it does not get in until we say so.

56
00:04:27,216 --> 00:04:31,166
Give the box one extra input for that, and call it load.

57
00:04:31,166 --> 00:04:36,079
When load gives the signal, the box takes in whatever is at its input,

58
00:04:36,079 --> 00:04:39,026
and from then on shows that at its output.

59
00:04:39,126 --> 00:04:44,786
At all other times its input can do anything, and its output stays put.

60
00:04:45,216 --> 00:04:49,780
A circuit like this, one that remembers, is called a register.

61
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:54,160
Its input is labelled D and its output Q.

62
00:04:54,616 --> 00:04:57,298
Who gives the load signal, and when?

63
00:04:57,298 --> 00:05:04,690
We want exactly one load for each number, so it should come at the same rhythm as the numbers themselves.

64
00:05:04,790 --> 00:05:09,596
In our example that is once every eight nanoseconds.

65
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:13,482
A computer has a signal with precisely that job.

66
00:05:13,482 --> 00:05:20,752
It is called the clock, and it does nothing but go high and low, high and low, at a steady rate.

67
00:05:20,852 --> 00:05:25,684
It is made on the motherboard and wired to every part of the chip.

68
00:05:26,116 --> 00:05:30,429
The moment it goes from low to high is called a rising edge,

69
00:05:30,429 --> 00:05:34,166
and the moment it comes back down is a falling edge.

70
00:05:34,266 --> 00:05:38,748
The time from one rising edge to the next is one clock period.

71
00:05:38,748 --> 00:05:41,196
Ours is eight nanoseconds.

72
00:05:41,196 --> 00:05:48,276
In a real processor it is closer to one nanosecond, which is a billion ticks every second.

73
00:05:48,716 --> 00:05:51,630
Now connect the clock to the load input.

74
00:05:51,630 --> 00:05:57,870
This register takes in a new value at every rising edge, and only then.

75
00:05:59,116 --> 00:06:01,038
Let's run the list again.

76
00:06:01,038 --> 00:06:07,922
First the register has to start at zero, and registers have an input for that, called reset.

77
00:06:08,022 --> 00:06:15,958
If reset is one at a rising edge, the register clears to zero, whatever is waiting at D.

78
00:06:16,416 --> 00:06:22,898
The register shows zero and the first number is three, so the adder settles on three.

79
00:06:22,998 --> 00:06:29,332
And this time the three just waits at the register's input, because no edge has come yet.

80
00:06:29,783 --> 00:06:31,485
Here is the edge.

81
00:06:31,485 --> 00:06:38,355
The register takes the three, the list moves on to one, and the adder settles on four.

82
00:06:38,455 --> 00:06:44,435
At the next edge the register takes the four, the list gives four, and the adder says eight.

83
00:06:44,435 --> 00:06:48,799
One more edge, and eight plus two is ten.

84
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:52,264
At the following edge the ten is stored.

85
00:06:52,264 --> 00:06:56,784
Four numbers, four ticks, and the total is ten.

86
00:06:56,884 --> 00:07:00,335
The circuit now takes one step per clock period,

87
00:07:00,335 --> 00:07:05,082
and the register is what holds each step back until the next tick.

88
00:07:06,316 --> 00:07:11,411
So a circuit that feeds its own output back needs something that holds a value still,

89
00:07:11,411 --> 00:07:13,748
and something that says when to let go.

90
00:07:13,748 --> 00:07:17,138
Those are the register and the clock.

91
00:07:17,238 --> 00:07:22,091
A circuit built this way, where every change follows a clock edge,

92
00:07:22,091 --> 00:07:24,958
is called a synchronous digital system.

93
00:07:25,416 --> 00:07:28,268
We were generous to the register, though.

94
00:07:28,268 --> 00:07:34,462
In our picture it took its new value in no time at all, exactly at the edge.

95
00:07:34,562 --> 00:07:38,550
But a register is made of transistors, just like the adder.

96
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:43,118
So what is inside it, and what does at the edge really mean?

